# SDLC pipeline

Work flows through roles in order. Each role produces one artifact and hands off.

```
business-analyst  -> brd.md, frd.md
solution-architect -> hld.md, lld.md, adr/
qa-engineer        -> test-cases.md
project-manager    -> work-breakdown.md, execution-plan.md, GitHub issues
developer          -> code, tests, pull requests
debugger           -> defects/, fixes
```

Artifacts live in `docs/sdlc/<feature>/` in the repository being built. Identifiers are
stable and traceable: `BR-###` -> `FR-###` -> `C-###` -> `TC-###` -> `T-###`.

## Rules that hold across every role

- Requirements never name a technology. Designs never restate a requirement.
- Every downstream item traces to an upstream id. Something tracing to nothing is either
  scope creep or a missing requirement, and both get reported rather than absorbed.
- Tasks live in GitHub Issues, with estimates, phase and dependencies on the Projects v2
  board. `execution-plan.md` is generated from that data and must never be hand-edited.
  All task mutations go through the `sdlc-tracker` MCP tools.
- Tests come before implementation, and the failing test is confirmed to fail for the right
  reason before any code is written.
- Gates are soft: record an assumption and continue, but escalate a genuine conflict, an
  irreversible decision, or scope that grew beyond the approved artifact.

## Spec Kit

When `.specify/` exists in the repository, the `docs/sdlc/` artifacts are the upstream of
Spec Kit rather than a replacement for it: hand the FRD and designs to `/speckit.plan` and
`/speckit.tasks` instead of producing a competing plan.
